  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "Far away",
      "title": "Your users are far away",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Your servers are in Virginia. A user in London waits about 75 ms per round trip; in Sydney about 200 ms. A page needs many round trips, so Sydney waits well over a second.\n\nMeanwhile every image for every user worldwide is served by that one origin.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "You can’t speed up light. The only way to cut distance latency is to serve users from somewhere closer."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "80 ms",
        "syd": "210 ms",
        "origin": "9,000 req/s",
        "hitRate": "0%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Same bytes",
      "title": "Most bytes are the same for everyone",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Look inside a page load. The HTML is personal, but images, scripts, stylesheets and videos are **identical** for every user.\n\nIdentical, rarely-changing files are perfect for caching — anywhere, including far from the origin.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Static assets are often 90%+ of the bytes on a page."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "80 ms",
        "syd": "210 ms",
        "origin": "9,000 req/s",
        "hitRate": "0%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Edges",
      "title": "Put copies at the edge",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "A [[CDN]] (content delivery network) runs [[edge servers]] in hundreds of cities. Point your static files at it, and each user is served by the edge nearest them.\n\nLondon now gets images in ~10 ms. Only misses (amber) travel all the way to Virginia.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Popular CDNs: Cloudflare, Fastly, Akamai, Amazon CloudFront. You rent their edges; you don’t run them."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Edge servers in London and Sydney",
        "~ 90% of requests answered at the edge",
        "~ Origin load 9,000 → 900 req/s"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "12 ms",
        "syd": "15 ms",
        "origin": "900 req/s",
        "hitRate": "90%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Cold edge",
      "title": "First requests are misses",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Edges are [[pull-through]] caches: they start empty. The first request for a file misses, the edge fetches it from the origin, keeps a copy, and serves everyone after that.\n\nLondon’s edge is warm; Sydney’s just started and is still filling up — so Sydney users briefly pay the long trip.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "This is the same cache-aside idea from the last lesson, spread around the planet."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Sydney edge: cold, filling from origin",
        "~ Sydney latency 15 → 95 ms (for now)"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "12 ms",
        "syd": "95 ms",
        "origin": "2,400 req/s",
        "hitRate": "70%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "origin-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "el": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "es": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "le": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "se": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Old files",
      "title": "You ship a new logo. Nobody sees it.",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "You replace `logo.png` on the origin. But edges cached the old one with a one-day TTL, so users keep seeing the old logo — some in London, some in Sydney, for hours.\n\nYou could purge the edges — modern CDNs do that in seconds — but someone has to remember to, every time, and no purge can reach the copies already saved in users’ browsers.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Long TTLs are what make a CDN fast — and what makes updates slow to appear."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Origin: new logo.png",
        "- Edges still serve the old one"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "12 ms",
        "syd": "15 ms",
        "origin": "900 req/s",
        "hitRate": "90%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "origin-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "el": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "es": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "le": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "se": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Versioning",
      "title": "Give every version a new name",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Put a fingerprint of the file’s contents in its name: `logo.3f9a.png`. A new logo gets a new name, so it can never be confused with the old one.\n\nNow files can be cached for a **year**. The HTML (short TTL, or not cached) simply points at the latest names.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Build tools such as Vite and webpack add these content hashes for you."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ logo.png → logo.3f9a.png",
        "~ Cache lifetime 1 day → 1 year",
        "- Stale logos"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "12 ms",
        "syd": "15 ms",
        "origin": "900 req/s",
        "hitRate": "96%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "origin-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "el": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "es": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "le": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "se": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Spike",
      "title": "Spikes hit the edge, not you",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "A launch goes viral and traffic jumps 20×. Almost all of it is answered by edges spread around the world; the origin barely notices.\n\nThe same spread-out capacity helps absorb floods of malicious traffic (DDoS attacks).",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "A CDN is a cache, a global network and a shield for your origin, all at once."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Traffic × 20",
        "~ Origin load stays ≈ 1,100 req/s"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "14 ms",
        "syd": "18 ms",
        "origin": "1,100 req/s",
        "hitRate": "97%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "lon": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "syd": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "origin-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "el": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "es": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "le": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "se": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Private",
      "title": "Case: never cache someone’s cart",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "A config change tells the CDN to cache *every* page for 5 minutes. Bob opens his cart; the edge stores it. Alice opens hers a second later — and the edge serves her **Bob’s cart**.\n\nPages that depend on who is asking must be marked `Cache-Control: private` (or `no-store`) so edges always pass them through to the origin.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Caching personal data publicly is a classic, very public incident. Default to private; cache public content on purpose."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Cart and account pages: Cache-Control: private",
        "~ Personal pages always go to the origin"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "12 ms",
        "syd": "15 ms",
        "origin": "1,400 req/s",
        "hitRate": "88%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "lon": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "syd": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "el": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "es": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "le": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "se": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Shield",
      "title": "Origin shield: one more layer",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "A real CDN has hundreds of edges. When a new file goes viral, every edge misses once — hundreds of requests for the same file hit your origin at the same moment.\n\nAn [[origin shield]] is a mid-tier cache in front of the origin. Edges miss to the shield; only the shield ever asks the origin, so each file is fetched once.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Most CDNs offer shielding as a setting. Pick a shield location close to your origin."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Origin shield between edges and origin",
        "~ Origin sees one fetch per file"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "12 ms",
        "syd": "15 ms",
        "origin": "120 req/s",
        "hitRate": "98%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "el": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "es": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "le": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "se": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "short": "Recap",
      "title": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "body": "• Distance adds latency; **edge servers** move content close to users.\n• CDNs are **pull-through caches**: misses fill them from your origin.\n• **Versioned file names** let you cache for a year and still update instantly.\n• The edge absorbs **spikes** and shields the origin.\n• Never cache **personal pages** publicly; add an **origin shield** at scale.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "That wraps up Scaling. Next module: **Data & Storage**, starting with SQL vs NoSQL."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "lon": "12 ms",
        "syd": "15 ms",
        "origin": "900 req/s",
        "hitRate": "96%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    }
  ]
